<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; margin: 0 auto; max-width: 900px; padding: 20px;">

    <h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        Lesson Planner – Theme # Technology in Education – Lesson # 5
    </h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic; margin-top: -10px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).
    </p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology in Education</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> When It's Better to Ignore Technology</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Facilitate a discussion on a group project. Concept: Simplify complex concepts for group comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate a discussion on technology use by encouraging classmates to share opinions (e.g., asks peers to share their favorite school app and explain why it's their favorite). Can simplify complex technology terms by using simple words and examples (e.g., explains Wi-Fi as the internet that connects phones and computers without wires). Can clarify technology terms using both English and familiar language to help peers (e.g., explains "download" in both languages if classmates are unsure).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; border-bottom: 2px solid #1a5276; padding-bottom: 5px; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying a simple image of a smartphone being used during a family dinner. Students, in pairs, briefly discuss for one minute: "When is it better to ignore technology?" using simple present for opinions. (e.g., "I think it is better to ignore my phone when I am eating.")</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to simplify a complex concept. For example, the teacher writes "Cloud Storage" on the board and says: "Cloud storage means saving your files not on your computer, but on the internet. It's like having a digital locker online where you can keep your photos and documents, and access them from any device. It's really useful!" The teacher demonstrates clear pronunciation with stress on words like "digital" and "online."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of 'Cloud Storage' and the concept of simplifying.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is Cloud Storage on your physical computer or on the internet?" (Internet)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I explain a difficult word, do I use more difficult words or simpler words?" (Simpler words)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology in Education".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short informational text (genre: instructional, register: semi-formal) titled "Key Terms for Our Classroom Tech Debate." This text outlines 3-4 slightly complex technology terms (e.g., "digital footprint," "algorithm," "data privacy") and 2-3 guidelines for a constructive debate (e.g., "Always use evidence," "Respect different opinions"). The terms are aligned with the target vocabulary such as `network`, `platform`, `system`, and verbs like `browse`, `synchronize`.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in conversations on the appropriate use of technology, offering suggestions for limiting or incorporating it in school.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text individually.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is this text mainly about?" "Why are these terms important for a debate?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match each complex tech term from the text to a general meaning provided in a list.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 minutes</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Simple present for expressing opinions and comparing preferences. Modal "should" for suggestions and "can" for ability. Wh-questions for discussions.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> `technology`, `apps`, `tools`, `benefit`, `problem`, `solution`, `device`, `platform`, `software`, `use`, `ignore`, `prefer`, `advise`, `recommend`, `appropriate`, `useful`, `beneficial`, `innovative`, `educational`, `accessible`, `while`, `but`, `instead`, `because`.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Focus on stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "education," "innovative," "software"). Intonation patterns with "should" and "can."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Weighing benefits and drawbacks, suggesting limits, expressing opinions and comparing preferences.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                        <ol>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Simplification Match:</b> Students receive a worksheet with the complex tech terms from the presentation text and a list of simplified definitions. They draw lines to match each term with its simple explanation. (e.g., "digital footprint" -> "all the things you do online"). Teacher circulates, monitoring pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "digital" and "information."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Suggestion Rephrasing:</b> In pairs, students get cards with sentences like: "Technology can sometimes make us distracted." They practice rephrasing these into suggestions using "should" or "can" and discourse markers, focusing on clear intonation. For example, "We should limit our screen time while studying, because it can be distracting." or "We can use an app blocker instead of constantly checking social media."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Clarifying Questions:</b> Students read short statements about technology use (e.g., "I prefer to use a physical textbook instead of an e-book for reading.") and then practice asking their partner Wh-questions to understand their reasoning. (e.g., "Why do you prefer physical textbooks, and what benefits do they offer over e-books?"). The listener then tries to summarize their partner's opinion to check understanding.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology in Education".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Group Debate Preparation & Mediation Role-Play</b>
                    Students are divided into small groups for their "Classroom Tech Debate" project. Each group is assigned a role (e.g., 'Pro-Technology,' 'Anti-Technology,' 'Balanced Approach'). Within each group, one student takes on the role of a "Mediator" for a specific part of the preparation. This mediator's task is to:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Simplify Concepts:</b> Explain one complex argument or tech term (e.g., "digital citizenship," "gamified learning") clearly to their group members, ensuring everyone understands it for the debate. They can use simple words and examples, or even familiar language if necessary (as per the learning outcome).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Facilitate Discussion:</b> Encourage classmates to share their opinions on a debate point (e.g., "What apps do you like the most for school, and why do you think they're beneficial?"). They use Wh-questions and modals ('should'/'can') to guide the conversation and suggest limits or benefits of technology.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Summarize Key Points:</b> At the end of their mediation turn, they briefly summarize the group's main arguments or ideas discussed.</li>
                    </ul>
                    The teacher provides scenario cards for the mediator to choose from (e.g., "Explain the concept of 'screen addiction' to your group," "Lead a discussion on whether phones should be allowed during breaks," "Clarify the term 'learning management system' (LMS) for your peers").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in clarifying, simplifying, and encouraging discussion related to technology in education. Effective use of `should`, `can`, Wh-questions, and target vocabulary for mediation.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes each student acting as a mediator in their small groups during the debate preparation phase. Students who are not mediating are peer-assessing their group's mediator using a simple checklist.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation & Peer Checklist):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Explanation:</b> Did the student simplify complex concepts effectively? (e.g., explains Wi-Fi as the internet that connects phones and computers without wires).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Facilitation of Discussion:</b> Did the student encourage peers to share opinions using Wh-questions? (e.g., asks peers to share their favorite school app and explain why it's their favorite).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Did the student use modals ("should," "can") and relevant vocabulary (`benefit`, `problem`, `solution`, `recommend`, `appropriate`) correctly?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation and Intonation:</b> Was their speech generally intelligible, with appropriate stress and intonation for questions and suggestions?</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new strategy I used today to explain a difficult idea to my classmates was..."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I think I can improve my mediation skills by..."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of mediating the debate preparation, and how did I try to solve it?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can effective mediation skills help me in other school projects or outside of school?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in facilitating students' mediation skills today? The role-play in Stage 4 seemed particularly engaging, as students actively practiced simplifying and facilitating."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next time this lesson or a similar one is taught? Some students struggled with spontaneously forming Wh-questions to facilitate discussion; more explicit sentence frames might be useful. Also, ensuring all students get a chance to be the 'mediator' is crucial."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Technology in Education" theme. The mediation skills practiced today are crucial for students to consolidate their learning from the entire unit and present their full "Classroom Tech Debate" in a future cross-curricular project or a final presentation, applying their ability to clarify, explain, and discuss complex topics.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>